1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Early Diabetes Risk Screening: can self-reported survey answers find adults with diabetes or prediabetes?

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:14,000
253,680 adults, 21 answers each. Goal: catch 80% of cases while flagging as few people as possible.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Main outcome: prediabetes or diabetes (15.8% of people). Sensitivity check: prediabetes alone (2.1%).

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:29,000
One locked 80/20 split. Tuning, model choice and the cutoff used training rows only.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Every tuned model beats the paper risk checklist; XGBoost wins all five validation folds.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The screening cutoff (0.153) was locked on training data before the test set was opened.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Demo: the test command refuses to run until a cutoff has been locked for this exact split.

8
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Demo: locking the cutoff on the 202,944 training rows (the test rows stay locked).

9
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Demo: scoring the locked test set once. XGBoost finds 79.4% of cases and flags 37.6% of people.

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Demo: the committed model and cutoff score two made-up people.

11
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:27,000
On 50,736 unseen people: 79.4% of cases found, 37.6% flagged, 96 fewer false alarms per 1,000 than the checklist.

12
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:35,000
It misses more younger and higher-income people, and finds prediabetes (65%) less often than diabetes (81%).

13
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Self-reported, single-year survey data: a research prototype, not a diagnostic tool.

14
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Fully reproducible: one command per stage and 63 automated tests. Code and reports are on GitHub.
